  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Environment, Stakeholders, and Perceptions Analysis for Delivering Humanitarian Response in North-East Nigeria</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ability of humanitarian agencies to address some issues relevant to the humanitarian operation is limited, whether these relate to the operating environment or their ability to reach people in need. The reasons for this may be manifold and may stem from a lack of understanding of who key stakeholders are and how these stakeholders engage in policy-making and/or other decisions with a bearing on humanitarian operations. Some of the issues where improved stakeholder engagement is needed, include but are not limited to: protection concerns of civilians; access issues; bureaucratic impediments; and other issues relevant to the operating environment for humanitarian agencies.Similarly, there are gaps in understanding these stakeholders&apos; perceptions of humanitarian actors &#x2013; and how these perceptions may impact such decisions or the stance/positions that these stakeholders adopt vis-&#xe0;-vis humanitarian actors. This said, perceptions may, moreover, vary according to the nature and mandate of humanitarian actors, ranging from the Red Cross/Red Crescent movement through international and national/local NGOs to the United Nations system. Some of the issues where improved stakeholder engagement is needed, include but are not limited to: protection concerns of civilians; access issues; bureaucratic impediments; and other issues relevant to the operating environment for humanitarian agencies.</narrative>
  </description>
